uppressive and they were  marginally suppressive all of them right  but they were all pretty comparable but  again the mixtures were not more weed  suppressive than our most weed  suppressive monocultures all right so  that brings us to the last one last  question how do mixtures and  monocultures affect the growth of a pest  crop and this is the question that I was  most interested in right this this is  this is integrating across a lot of  potential functions to influence the  next crop how does the next crop respond  to these mixtures that's really what we  probably care about most particularly  farmers right now is this influencing my  cash crop potentially so here's the  results for the first experiment you can  see so this is this is canola and wheat  biomass total biomass right so we  separated them out but this is just  total test crop biomass you can see that  biomass of the test crop was not too bad  following the weedy fallow actually  really quite low following oats sorghum  and it was highest following buck wheat  so buckwheat monoculture resulted in the  highest test crop biomass and both of  our mixtures resulted in lower test crop  biomass  lower test crop performance compared to  when that followed buckwheat yep  weed weed weed biomass yep I would say  upwards of 8 or 9 but dominated by 2 or  3 species and we have those data for  sure I can't pull them up off the top of  my head but a lot of lambs quarter and  pigweed and maybe some crabgrass as kind  of the dominant species  so here's experiment 2 in this case  forage radish monoculture resulted in  the highest pest crop biomass our  six-way was lower than forage radish in  terms of the resulting test crop but the  14 weigh actually was statistically  similar to that forage radish but again  nothing higher than the best-performing  monoculture some cases lower but often  similar last experiment experiment  number three hairy vetch resulted in the  highest test crop biomass and in this  case both of the mixtures resulted in  pest crop biomass that was lower than  when it followed hairy vetch  and so I wanted to put this into some  context right so this is one expert well  three experiments at one site and so I  did kind of a I came up with this great  idea to do a quick literature review  yesterday or day before yesterday so I  didn't have a whole lot of time so this  is not a this is not an exhaustive  review of the literature but I'd  certainly been to enough scientific  meetings in the past couple of years  where I was hearing the same kinds of  results being presented that that the  mixtures not necessarily a whole lot  worse than the monocultures but not a  whole lot of evidence that they were  having a whole lot of benefits beyond  what we are seeing in the monocultures  and so I pulled out these sets of  studies that I could find you can find  this it's actually been quite a few of  these types of studies reported in the  literature very recently this has been  like I said it's been a pretty hot topic  and so I kind of went through the three  the three functions that I was  interested in this study cover crop  biomass weed suppression and then this  kind of carryover effect to the next  crop and and kind of ranked the  performance of the mixture relative to  the best-performing  monoculture which is kind of how I was  thinking about our study and you can see  that in most cases not everybody  measured everything but usually the  result was that cover crop in terms of  biomass it was either equal or less than  the best-performing monoculture in that  particular study that was by far the  overwhelming results in at least this  set of studies that I was able to find  and I could really only find one study  that reported anything suggestive of a  greater benefit in this case in terms of  cover crop biomass in the mixture  compared to the best-performing  monocultures so I think the results that  we're reporting here in our study are in  line with what a num